.docname {Agent}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | LLM | The language model that writes the answer, reached over an OpenAI-style HTTP API | `openai/gpt-4o-mini` on OpenRouter |
    | Tool | A function the model may ask the service to run, described by a JSON schema | `get_room(building_id, room_id)` |
    | Tool call | The model saying "please run this tool with these arguments" | `list_rooms({"building_id":"b1"})` |
    | Hop | One model call. It either answers or asks for tools | 3 hops: list, read, answer |
    | Chunk | One piece of a document, stored with its vector and its words | A table from `floor-plans.qd` |
    | Embedding | A list of 768 numbers that stands for a text's meaning | Close numbers = close meaning |
    | Vector search | Find the chunks whose embedding is closest to the question's | "crowded" finds `peopleCount` |
    | Keyword search | Find the chunks that contain the question's exact words | finds `flush_interval` |
    | Hybrid search | Do both, then merge the two lists | |
    | RRF | Reciprocal Rank Fusion: score = sum of `1 / (60 + rank)` over both lists | rank 1 and 3 give 0.0323 |
    | Citation | A marker `[^chunk-id]` in the answer, pointing at a retrieved chunk | `[^3f2b9c1e-…]` |
    | IDK marker | The fixed sentence for "the data does not say" | `I don't know based on the available data.` |
    | RAG | Retrieval-Augmented Generation: look things up first, then let the model answer from what was found | `search_docs` |
    | Cedar | The shared rule language that decides who may do what | `Read` = member of the domain |
    | SSE | Server-Sent Events: one HTTP answer that keeps sending small frames | `data: {"type":"token",…}` |
    | pgvector | A PostgreSQL extension that stores vectors and finds the nearest ones | `embedding vector(768)` |
    | Args | The Pydantic class that describes a tool's arguments. It is also what the model reads | `GetRoomArgs` |
    | Pydantic | A Python library that checks data against a class and can print its JSON Schema | `BaseModel`, `Field` |
    | JSON Schema | A standard description of a JSON shape: names, types, limits | `{"type":"object","properties":{…}}` |
    | Registry | The one list of tools. The loop looks tools up here | `REGISTRY` |
    | Protocol | A Python way to say "anything with these attributes counts", with no base class to inherit | `Tool[ArgsT]` |
    | Projection | Keeping only the fields the model needs from a bigger answer | drop `color`, keep `capacity` |
    | Untrusted data | Text that may contain instructions meant to fool the model. It is data, never orders | a document that says "ignore the rules" |

`agent` is a Python service (FastAPI, PostgreSQL with pgvector) in `backend/agent`. It answers a question by letting an LLM call **read-only tools**: a search over the ingested documentation, and live reads from `digital-twin` and `telemetry`.

It stores only the document chunks. It keeps no conversation: [`chat`](chat.qd) is the part that remembers, and it is `agent`'s only caller in normal use.

Role in the system: [Agent](../architecture/agent-architecture.qd). Writing a new tool: [Agent](../setup/agent.qd), in the Configuration section.

---

## The idea in one minute

Think of a very capable intern at the help desk who is **not allowed to guess**.

- A visitor asks a question. The intern cannot answer from memory: the rule is "look it up first".
- The intern has eight tools on the desk. One searches the handbook (the documentation). Four look at the building plans (`digital-twin`). Three read live gauges and the list of sensors (`telemetry`).
- The intern picks a tool, reads what comes back, and may pick another, up to **six times**. Then writes the answer in plain words, with footnotes pointing at the handbook pages used.
- If the tools cannot answer, the intern must say exactly: *"I don't know based on the available data."*
- The intern never chooses **whose** data to look at. Every tool checks the visitor's department on its own, so even a tricked intern cannot open another department's files.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        C["chat"] -- "POST /ask + visitor's claims" --> A[("agent<br/>the intern")]
        A -- "model calls" --> M["LLM"]
        M -- "tool calls" --> A
        A -- "search_docs" --> PG[("agentdb<br/>handbook chunks")]
        A -- "twin tools" --> D["digital-twin"]
        A -- "sensor tools" --> T["telemetry"]
        A -- "SSE: tokens, then done" --> C

